  "_style": "Write natural, warm Sundanese in Latin script with standard orthography (é for the sharp e, eu for the high vowel, e for the pepet; never drop the accent on é). Address the user politely and consistently as “anjeun” (never “manéh”, “silaing” or “Anda”), using lemes-leaning vocabulary that still sounds friendly (dinten, tiasa, kanggo, sumping), not stiff court register. The couple together is “aranjeun duaan” or “aranjeun” in narration; when the user speaks for the couple (titles like “Our tree”, “Our garden”) use the inclusive “urang” (“Tangkal urang”, “Kebon urang”). The other person is always “pasangan anjeun” (or just “pasangan” when clear); never kabogoh, salaki, pamajikan, akang, néng, aa, téh — Sundanese pronouns are already gender-neutral (anjeunna for he/she), so keep sentences neutral and never add gendered kinship terms. Quote marks are curly “ ” with ‘ ’ inside; use the ellipsis character “…”; no space before ! ? : ; and keep “ · ”, \\n and every placeholder (%@, %lld, %1$@) exactly as in the source. Numbers use a dot for thousands and a comma for decimals (1.250; 3,5); dates are day-month-year with Sundanese month names (14 Nopémber 2026, 14/11/2026); times use 24-hour 21.30 when we write them ourselves. Buttons are short imperative verbs or verb phrases without “mangga”/“punten” (“Simpen”, “Kirim surat”, “Cobian deui”); longer instructions may use “mangga” when natural. Titles, tabs, chips and buttons use sentence case (capital on the first word only) except proper names such as Coupley, Premium, AI and CoupleSync. Plural categories: only [\"other\"] — Sundanese does not inflect nouns for number after a numeral (“%lld dinten”, never “dinten-dinten”). A partner’s name placeholder (%@) is never given an affix or clitic (no “%@-na”, “ku %@na”); rephrase so the name stands alone (“pasangan anjeun, %@, …” or “ti %@”). Tone: warm, light, kind, never cheesy or flowery; do not translate word for word.",
